  "client_view_text": " A message that changes his life forever.\nThe lighthouse keeper, Jack, had been stationed on the rocky coast for five years. He had grown accustomed to the solitude, the rhythmic sound of the waves, and the occasional visit from a passing ship. But on this particular day, something caught his eye. A bottle, half-buried in the sand, caught the morning sun and glinted like a tiny jewel. Jack walked over to investigate, his boots sinking into the damp sand.\n\nAs he picked up the bottle, he noticed it was old, the glass worn and weathered. He carefully removed the cork and pulled out A message that changes his life forever.\nThe lighthouse keeper, Jack, had been stationed on the rocky coast for five years. He had grown accustomed to the solitude, the rhythmic sound of the waves, and the occasional visit from a passing ship. But on this particular day, something caught his eye. A bottle, half-buried in the sand, caught the morning sun and glinted like a tiny jewel. Jack walked towards it, curiosity getting the better of him.\n\nAs he picked up the bottle, he noticed it was old, the glass worn and weathered. He carefully removed the cork and pulled out a piece of paper, yellowed with age. Unfolding it, he saw that it was a letter, written in a flowing script.\n\n\"Dear whoever finds this,\" the letter began, \"I am writing to you from the ship 'Eclipse', bound for New York. I am a young woman, traveling with my family to start a new life. We set sail from Liverpool on the 15th of April, 1850. I am writing this on the 20th, and I fear we may be lost. The storm that has been brewing for days has grown fierce, and I fear for our safety. I can only"
}